      "math_academy_id": "e-5965",
      "problem": "Find the family of surfaces that describe the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y, z) = \\ln(x^2 + 2y^2 - z^2).$",
      "worked_solution": "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}) & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & ={e}^{c} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{c}}+\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{{e}^{c}}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{e}^{c}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get a family of hyperboloids of one sheet:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{e^c} + \\dfrac{y^2}{e^c/2} - \\dfrac{z^2}{e^c} =  1, \\qquad c \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$",
